last time we talked about bohmian  mechanics a possible alternative to  quantum mechanics one of the appeals of  bohmian mechanics is that we can always  at any time say that this is the  position of our particle on the other  hand with quantum mechanics the  superposition principle forces us to say  things like the particle is in many  different states at the same time with  bohmian mechanics it's as though reality  properly exists regardless of whether  we're measuring it or not or does it  today we're going to look at a  potentially big problem with bohmian  mechanics the issue is something called  contextual ax t let me explain it with  any sensible Theory you want measurement  to reveal a property that the particle  already had not create or change it I  should point out that under this  condition quantum mechanics is clearly  not sensible but from what we saw last  time it seems like bohmian mechanics is  so if you measure say the spin of a  particle in the Y direction and you find  it to be spin up in bohmian mechanics  you want to be able to say then that the  particle had spin up beforehand but what  about the case where there's more than  one way to measure the spin of a  particle another sensible sensibility  criteria is that different ways to  measure something should agree so both  those measurements should give the same  answer we'll call this non contextual ax  T because the result of the measurement  shouldn't depend on the experimental  context this is where the problem is  though in bohmian mechanics this doesn't  always happen so instead of always being  able to say statements like this  particle has spin up in the Y direction  you're instead reduced to saying things  like this particle has spin up in the Y  direction if you measure it this way but  spin down if you measure it another way  it's still perfectly deterministic  there's no randomness but it's just not  that nice this is what we call  contextual ax t  let me show you an example of this  happening this example is going to be  about the spin of an electron don't  panic if you've forgotten all about spin  I'm  they're going to use some very basic  facts about it if you want a refresher  on spin though you can go watch this  video about it first remember that every  electron has its pin pointing in one  particular direction however it's also  possible to measure the spin of an  electron in any direction and this is  where it gets weird whatever direction  you pick there are only two possible  values you can get which we call spin up  and spin down for reasons that you'll  see soon if you're feeling uncomfortable  about spin at this point feel free to  watch that video and ask me questions  back to spin though there's a pretty  standard way to measure the spin of a  particle it's called the stern-gerlach  machine you set it up to measure spin  along a certain axis let's say the y  axis then what happens is electrons go  through this passage I won't go into how  this works but if your particle happened  to be one that already had spin up in  the y axis it will get deflected up and  get measured around here if your  particle started with spin down in the Y  axis then it will get deflected down how  does bohmian mechanics explain this  remember that Imbaba mechanics there is  a quantum force that depends on the  wavefunction well spin is one of the  things that influence the wavefunction  so the quantum force takes spin into  account and then pushes the electron in  the correct directions to get these  cases of course most electrons have  their spin in other directions what  about if a bunch of electrons happen to  have their spin in the X direction which  is perpendicular to Y as we saw in the  spin video when you measure these  particles in the Y Direction half of  them will have spin up and half of them  will have spin down again bohmian  mechanics needs to explain this first we  need to deal with something there is no  real randomness in Fermi mechanics only  randomness from ignorance so we need to  be able to explain why half the  particles go one way and half the other  this is how it's done  let's say all the electrons set us  somewhere inside this little sphere but  we don't know exactly where inside if  one of these particles starts off in the  top half you can calculate by bohmian  mechanics that it will deflect up and  similarly for the bottom half giving  exactly the right result no issues so  far now let's look at sensibility  condition number one again we measure  that this particle starts here and has  spin up in the Y direction so our  condition tells us that it must have had  spin up in that direction all along  again this seems fairly innocuous the  problem is that there's another way to  measure the spin of that particular  particle why not set the stern-gerlach  machine to measure spin in the negative  y direction instead what should happen  well we've already decided that this  particle has spin up in the Y direction  so by the properties of spin it must  have spin down in the negative direction  sorry that's the last fact that I'll use  from that spin video so that means that  our particle has spin down in the  negative y direction so it's deflected  down now right not that's not what  bohmian mechanics says it still goes up  which forces us to conclude that it has  spin down in the Y direction and now we  see how our second sensibility condition  is broken if we measure the first way  our particle will look like it had spin  up but if we measure the second way it  will look like it had spin down notice  that I'm not saying bohmian mechanics  makes the wrong experimental prediction  because in the second case we also  expect that half the electrons deflect  up and half deflect down and that's  exactly what happens when your mechanics  can give you the right predictions on  any stern-gerlach experiment the issue  is much more subtle than that if we  can't say our particle has property X  without adding the proviso so long as  you measure it this way then what  property does the particle have before  anyone decides how to measure it I mean  this seems like a quantum mechanics  level philosophical problem  should we scrap bohmian mechanics and  look for some other better alternative  to quantum not so fast in fact it's  possible to prove that for any theory  that has sensibility condition number  one that experiments reveal pre-existing  properties then our second condition  that properties don't depend on the type  of experiment will always be broken at  least whenever you're talking about spin  in other words there are no completely  sensible theories there's a lot of hype  about this contextual 'ti but I'm not  sure how seriously we should take it you  see this issue doesn't come up at all  when we're talking about most variables  in bohmian mechanics like position  momentum and energy it's perfectly  legitimate to say that this particle has  this position regardless of how you  measure it the problem only comes when  you try and say statements like that  about spin and other similar variables  that's why some people say the theory  isn't broken only the way we think about  spin is they say  spin is not a real property of a  particle like position and momentum  instead it only comes into play in an  indirect way the spin affects the  wavefunction which affects the quantum  force which changes the direction of the  particle it doesn't make sense to say a  particle has spin up instead what you  mean is the particle experiences some  extra quantum force upwards because of  spin I'm pretty conflicted about this  and I'd love to hear your opinion about  all of this  does bohmian mechanics escape or is this  really the final blow for all sensible  alternatives to quantum mechanics I  don't know but I'd like to leave you  with one deep and meaningful thought  though spin is weird

